1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,390
On April 16th, 1991, Bernie Madoff said he bought 123 call options

2
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:08,250
on Procter and Gamble stock.

3
00:00:08,730 --> 00:00:12,810
One problem, in the entire market that whole day, only 20 of

4
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:14,610
those options changed hands.

5
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:18,470
He claimed six times more than existed, and nobody stopped him for

6
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:19,830
17 more years.

7
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:24,090
By the time it collapsed, the fake balances said $65 billion.

8
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:26,170
Here's the thing.

9
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:28,330
That impossible number isn't a fluke.

10
00:00:28,390 --> 00:00:32,150
The biggest frauds in history all hide one, a number that cannot

11
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:32,910
exist.

12
00:00:33,470 --> 00:00:34,510
Today, three of them.

13
00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:38,590
A coupon scam from 1920, a tank farm full of water, and

14
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:40,410
the fund that almost never lost.

15
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:43,630
And by the end, you'll have a three question test that would

16
00:00:43,690 --> 00:00:46,750
have caught all three, because the next one won't call itself a

17
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:47,830
Ponzi scheme.

18
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:51,550
Chapter one, the coupon that couldn't exist.

19
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:56,470
Boston, 1920, banks are paying about 5 % a year, and a

20
00:00:56,530 --> 00:01:02,070
former dishwasher named Charles Ponzi is promising 50 % in 45 days.

21
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:03,870
His pitch sounded clever.

22
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:08,590
International reply coupons, little slips you could buy cheap in Europe, where

23
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:12,230
money was weak after the war, and trade in America for stamps

24
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:13,170
worth more.

25
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:15,070
Buy low there, cash in high here.

26
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:16,830
On paper, that's arbitrage.

27
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:18,070
It's even legal.

28
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:20,470
And for a while, it looked like it worked.

29
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:22,910
Early investors got paid on time.

30
00:01:23,070 --> 00:01:26,710
Word spread, by July, money was pouring in at close to a

31
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:28,130
million dollars a day.

32
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:30,990
Even most of Boston's police force bought in.

33
00:01:31,210 --> 00:01:35,470
But nobody did the math, until a financial journalist named Clarence Barron

34
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:36,130
did.

35
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:41,590
To cover what Ponzi owed, 160 million postal coupons would have to

36
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:42,710
be in circulation.

37
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:45,950
The actual number, about 27 ,000.

38
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:47,630
That's not a little short.

39
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:50,870
That's about one coupon for every 6 ,000 he'd need.

40
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:52,950
So where were the profits coming from?

41
00:01:53,010 --> 00:01:54,530
The next investor.

42
00:01:54,730 --> 00:01:57,670
Old investors were paid with new investors' money.

43
00:01:57,970 --> 00:01:58,970
That's the whole trick.

44
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:02,810
When the papers ran the numbers, the line outside his office turned

45
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:03,770
into a run.

46
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:05,250
Six banks went down.

47
00:02:05,870 --> 00:02:07,570
Investors lost about $20 million.

48
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:11,990
In 1920, Ponzi pleaded guilty to mail fraud.

49
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,370
His name became the word for the scam.

50
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:17,390
And he died broke with an estate of $75.

51
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:21,170
Ponzi's impossible number was hiding in coupons.

52
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:25,070
The next one was hiding in plain sight, inside a tank.

53
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:28,430
Chapter two, more oil than America.

54
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:31,470
New Jersey, the early 1960s.

55
00:02:31,590 --> 00:02:36,490
Tino De Angelis runs a vegetable oil company called Allied Crude, out

56
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:38,950
of a run -down tank farm in Bayonne.

57
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:41,670
His business ran on a simple kind of loan.

58
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:43,070
Oil sits in a tank.

59
00:02:43,190 --> 00:02:46,170
A warehouse company checks the tank and writes a receipt.

60
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:48,250
Banks lend money against the receipt.

61
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:50,090
And the company, writing those receipts.

62
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:52,410
A unit of American Express.

63
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:55,890
If American Express vouched for the oil, the banks paid.

64
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:58,190
So De Angelis made more oil.

65
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:00,570
Not real oil, receipt oil.

66
00:03:00,990 --> 00:03:05,010
Some tanks had hidden compartments, so inspectors saw oil at the hatch.

67
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:07,790
Others were mostly water, because oil floats.

68
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:10,890
Inspectors looked at the top, saw oil and signed.

69
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:13,930
And when they walked to the next tank, pipes moved the same

70
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:15,170
oil ahead of them.

71
00:03:15,230 --> 00:03:17,370
The same oil, count again and again.

72
00:03:17,790 --> 00:03:19,110
Here's the impossible number.

73
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:25,150
By 1963, Allied claimed more soybean and cottonseed oil than existed in

74
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:26,950
the entire United States.

75
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:29,570
On paper, 900 ,000 tons.

76
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:32,490
Actually there, about 55 ,000.

77
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:34,790
Over 90 % of it never existed.

78
00:03:35,130 --> 00:03:37,710
Then he tried to corner the market in oil futures.

79
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:43,410
When prices cracked in November 1963, the margin calls came and somebody

80
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:45,490
finally looked inside the tanks.

81
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:50,270
51 lenders, more than $180 million in losses.

82
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:53,290
The American Express warehouse unit went bankrupt.

83
00:03:53,510 --> 00:03:55,570
A Wall Street brokerage went under.

84
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:59,350
De Angelis pleaded guilty and was sentenced to 20 years.

85
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:00,810
He was out in seven.

86
00:04:01,410 --> 00:04:06,030
Coupons, tanks, both frauds needed more of something than the world actually

87
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:06,630
had.

88
00:04:06,690 --> 00:04:10,210
But the biggest one in history needed something even rarer.

89
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:11,370
A perfect record.

90
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:15,230
Chapter three, the fund that almost never lost.

91
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:17,490
Bernie Madoff wasn't a nobody.

92
00:04:17,710 --> 00:04:19,430
He'd been chairman of the NASDAQ.

93
00:04:19,610 --> 00:04:22,910
His trading firm was one of the biggest market makers on Wall

94
00:04:22,970 --> 00:04:23,490
Street.

95
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:27,410
And his investment fund had a pitch that sounded almost boring.

96
00:04:27,690 --> 00:04:32,190
No 50%, just steady returns around 10 % a year, year after

97
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:33,490
year after year.

98
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:35,350
That was the genius of it.

99
00:04:35,530 --> 00:04:36,570
Ponzi sold greed.

100
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:38,210
Madoff sold safety.

101
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:42,630
One fund reported 10 .5 % a year for 17 years.

102
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:46,730
In late 2008, while markets were crashing, it said it was still

103
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:47,810
up for the year.

104
00:04:48,130 --> 00:04:50,270
Now picture a real investment chart.

105
00:04:50,350 --> 00:04:54,170
It wiggles, it drops, it has bad months and bad years.

106
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:58,490
Madoff's line went up at an almost perfect 45 degree angle.

107
00:04:58,810 --> 00:05:03,990
A financial analyst named Harry Markopolos was asked to copy Madoff's strategy.

108
00:05:04,110 --> 00:05:07,250
He later said he knew within five minutes that the numbers didn't

109
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:08,010
add up.

110
00:05:08,090 --> 00:05:10,110
It took him four hours to prove it.

111
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:11,730
His red flags were math.

112
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:15,990
Madoff reported losing months only about 4 % of the time.

113
00:05:16,090 --> 00:05:17,970
Real markets don't move like that.

114
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:20,390
And the strategy itself was impossible.

115
00:05:20,670 --> 00:05:24,110
To make the trades Madoff claimed, he would have needed more options

116
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:28,310
on the Chicago Board Options Exchange than actually existed.

117
00:05:28,630 --> 00:05:34,530
Same pattern as 1991, 123 options claimed, 20 traded.

118
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:38,270
Markopolos took his math to the SEC in 2000.

119
00:05:38,570 --> 00:05:43,410
And again, in 2005, he sent a memo titled, the world's largest

120
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:45,050
hedge fund is a fraud.

121
00:05:45,430 --> 00:05:50,010
Over 16 years, regulators looked at Madoff's firm at least eight times.

122
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:51,510
They didn't find the fraud.

123
00:05:51,930 --> 00:05:57,050
Then 2008 happened, markets crashed and investors asked for their money back.

124
00:05:57,110 --> 00:05:59,170
About $7 billion of it.

125
00:05:59,390 --> 00:06:03,730
Madoff's bank account, which had held over $5 billion earlier that year,

126
00:06:04,050 --> 00:06:06,950
was down to about 234 million.

127
00:06:07,310 --> 00:06:10,770
On December 10th, he told his sons he was finished and that

128
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:12,690
the fund was a Ponzi scheme.

129
00:06:12,910 --> 00:06:14,070
They went to the authorities.

130
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:16,350
The next morning, he was arrested.

131
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:21,610
Prosecutors put the fraud at $64 .8 billion based on the fake

132
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:22,450
statements.

133
00:06:22,730 --> 00:06:27,790
Markopolos estimated that 35 to 55 billion of that was profit that

134
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:29,230
never existed at all.

135
00:06:29,290 --> 00:06:34,090
Madoff pleaded guilty to 11 federal crimes and was sentenced to 150

136
00:06:34,150 --> 00:06:35,230
years.

137
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:37,490
He died in prison in 2021.

138
00:06:38,630 --> 00:06:40,710
So here's the impossible number test.

139
00:06:40,890 --> 00:06:43,490
Three questions, they would have caught all three.

140
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:47,410
Question one, does the math need more of something than exists?

141
00:06:47,730 --> 00:06:50,810
More coupons, more oil, more options.

142
00:06:51,050 --> 00:06:54,170
If a return needs a market bigger than the market, it's not

143
00:06:54,230 --> 00:06:54,990
a return.

144
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:57,730
Question two, is it too smooth?

145
00:06:57,890 --> 00:06:58,850
Real money wiggles.

146
00:06:58,910 --> 00:07:02,150
If something never has a bad month, that's not skill.

147
00:07:02,370 --> 00:07:03,210
That's a story.

148
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:06,950
Question three, can anyone check it besides them?

149
00:07:07,190 --> 00:07:09,370
Ponzi kept his books on index cards.

150
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:12,790
De Angelis's inspectors had close ties to him.

151
00:07:13,010 --> 00:07:16,430
Madoff ran his own brokerage, so he checked his own homework.

152
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:21,870
Three frauds, three different eras, one pattern, a number that can't exist.

153
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:25,770
So next time someone shows you a perfect chart, don't ask how

154
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:26,610
much you could make.

155
00:07:26,670 --> 00:07:29,010
Ask what it would take for it to be true.

156
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:32,570
Subscribe for the next case, and tell me in the comments which

157
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:34,590
red flag would have caught you first.

158
00:07:34,770 --> 00:07:37,350
Then watch the tank that fooled American Express.
